<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter13_multiple_inheritance_and_interface_composition">
    <title>Multiple Inheritance and Interface Composition</title>

    <p>
        C++ allows a class to inherit from more than one base class. This is called
        <term>multiple inheritance</term>. Multiple inheritance can be useful when
        a concrete class must satisfy several small, independent interfaces.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_multiple_base_classes">
        <title>More Than One Base Class</title>

        <p>
            In a multiple-inheritance declaration, the base classes are separated by
            commas. In this example, a <c>Report</c> is both printable and savable.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter13_multiple_interfaces">
            <title>A report implements two independent interfaces</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter13_multiple_interfaces"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Printable {
public:
    virtual void print() const = 0;
    virtual ~Printable() = default;
};

class Savable {
public:
    virtual void save() const = 0;
    virtual ~Savable() = default;
};

class Report : public Printable, public Savable {
public:
    void print() const override {
        std::cout << "Report printed\n";
    }

    void save() const override {
        std::cout << "Report saved\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Report report;
    report.print();
    report.save();

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The two base classes define separate responsibilities. The concrete
            <c>Report</c> class supplies both required operations. Each interface
            can also be used independently through a reference to its base type.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_interface_references">
        <title>Using Each Interface Separately</title>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter13_interface_references"
                 language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Printable {
public:
    virtual void print() const = 0;
    virtual ~Printable() = default;
};

class Savable {
public:
    virtual void save() const = 0;
    virtual ~Savable() = default;
};

class Report : public Printable, public Savable {
public:
    void print() const override {
        std::cout << "Printing report\n";
    }

    void save() const override {
        std::cout << "Saving report\n";
    }
};

void print_document(const Printable& document) {
    document.print();
}

void save_document(const Savable& document) {
    document.save();
}

int main() {
    Report report;
    print_document(report);
    save_document(report);

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The two functions accept only the interface they need. This keeps each
            function focused: <c>print_document()</c> does not need to know how to
            save a document, and <c>save_document()</c> does not need to know how to
            print one.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_multiple_inheritance_tradeoffs">
        <title>Benefits and Risks</title>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter13_multiple_inheritance_tradeoffs">
            <title>Multiple inheritance design considerations</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="37%"/>
                <col width="38%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><p>Consideration</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Potential benefit</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Potential risk</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Small interfaces</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>A class can satisfy several independent contracts.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Too many interfaces can make the class difficult to understand.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Shared implementation</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Inherited behavior may be reused.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Two bases may provide members with the same name.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Class hierarchy</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Different interfaces can be combined explicitly.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Complex hierarchies can create ambiguity and coupling.</p></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            Multiple inheritance is easiest to explain when the base classes are
            small interfaces with distinct responsibilities. When the goal is to
            combine implementation components, composition is often clearer and
            easier to maintain.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_ambiguous_members">
        <title>Ambiguous Members</title>

        <p>
            If two base classes provide members with the same name, a derived class
            may need to qualify the desired base explicitly. For example,
            <c>Printable::reset()</c> and <c>Savable::reset()</c> identify two
            different functions. This is one reason interfaces should have focused,
            nonconflicting responsibilities.
        </p>

        <p>
            Multiple inheritance does not mean that every class should inherit from
            many implementation-heavy classes. Prefer a small number of stable
            interfaces, and use composition when an object is simply made from
            reusable parts.
        </p>

        <p>
            The visualization shows one concrete object implementing two pure
            interfaces.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter13_multiple_inheritance"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aclass%20Printable%20%7B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20virtual%20void%20print%28%29%20const%20%3D%200%3B%0A%20%20%20%20virtual%20~Printable%28%29%20%3D%20default%3B%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aclass%20Savable%20%7B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20virtual%20void%20save%28%29%20const%20%3D%200%3B%0A%20%20%20%20virtual%20~Savable%28%29%20%3D%20default%3B%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aclass%20Report%20%3A%20public%20Printable%2C%20public%20Savable%20%7B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20void%20print%28%29%20const%20override%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Report%20printed%5Cn%22%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%0A%20%20%20%20void%20save%28%29%20const%20override%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Report%20saved%5Cn%22%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20Report%20report%3B%0A%20%20%20%20Printable%26%20printable%20%3D%20report%3B%0A%20%20%20%20Savable%26%20savable%20%3D%20report%3B%0A%20%20%20%20printable.print%28%29%3B%0A%20%20%20%20savable.save%28%29%3B%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=390&amp;codeDivWidth=420&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;curInstr=0&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="16:10">
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises_chapter13_multiple_inheritance">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_multiple_inheritance_1">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Why might a class inherit from both <c>Printable</c> and
                    <c>Savable</c>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Because every class must have two base classes.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Multiple inheritance is optional and should express a real design.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>Because the class must satisfy two independent interfaces.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The concrete class supplies both required operations.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Because composition is impossible in C++.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Composition is often a useful alternative.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Because pure virtual functions automatically create objects.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Pure virtual functions define required operations; they do not create objects.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_multiple_inheritance_multiple">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which statements about interface-style multiple inheritance are
                    correct? Select all that apply.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>A concrete class must implement inherited pure virtual functions.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Implementing the required operations makes the class concrete.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>A class can be viewed through either interface's base reference.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The object satisfies both public contracts.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Multiple inheritance always provides simpler code than composition.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Multiple inheritance can increase ambiguity and coupling.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>Small, independent interfaces are usually easier to combine safely.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Focused interfaces reduce conflicts and make responsibilities clearer.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_multiple_inheritance_2">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    When is composition often preferable to multiple inheritance?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    Composition is often preferable when the goal is to combine
                    implementation components rather than satisfy several stable
                    interfaces.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_multiple_inheritance_parsons"
                  number="yes"
                  adaptive="yes"
                  indentation="show">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Arrange the blocks to declare a class that implements both
                    interfaces.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <blocks randomize="yes">
                <block order="1">
                    <cline>class Report : public Printable, public Savable {</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="2">
                    <cline>public:</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="3">
                    <cline>    void print() const override;</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="4">
                    <cline>    void save() const override;</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="5">
                    <cline>};</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="1" correct="no">
                    <cline>class Report : private Printable {</cline>
                </block>
            </blocks>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</section>
